There is a fundamental difference. The priests you donate money to are
working in temples. They keep the money, supposedly to finance their temple
work (redistributing it to the poor ["two bits for an ale"] or whatever).
Gods are not interested in gold (why should they?). The gods just reward
you for your support of the local religious community.

The player, being on a divine quest, doesn't do anything related to
whatever it is a temple priest does when on duty, even if he belongs to the
priest class. So he can only make his contributions through a middle man.

So much for the "reasoning". Now for the balance issues of your suggestion:
While it is true that having the gold vanish is a disadvantage when
compared to normal donations, that only becomes an issue later in the game.
The possibility to get protection for money at any altar would make the
protection racket approach much easier: Credit clone a few shops and rob a
few vaults, then get your protection without ever going deeper than, say,
DL4 (with luck), or into such a dangerous place as the mines, at XL1. For a
class-specific ability, that is quite a lot of an advantage (on top of the
powerful BUC identification ability).

